(Q3) Why is Linux preferred for Mainframe Servers for legacy application? Give three
out-of-the-box technical reasons? 

             Three key technical reasons why Linux is preferred for mainframe servers running legacy applications:
        1. POSIX Compliance and Unix-Like Architecture
Linux follows POSIX (Portable Operating System Interface) standards, which makes it highly compatible with legacy Unix-based applications that were originally designed for mainframe environments.  Linux's Unix-like kernel architecture, system calls, and command-line interface provide a natural migration path without requiring extensive application rewrites or recompilation.
       2. Robust Memory Management and Process Isolation
Linux provides robust memory management with virtual memory systems that can handle the massive memory requirements typical of mainframe legacy applications. Its process isolation mechanisms ensure that critical legacy applications run in protected memory spaces, preventing one application failure from affecting others. 
      3. Extensive Hardware Abstraction and Driver Support
Linux offers excellent hardware abstraction layers that can interface with both modern and legacy mainframe hardware components. It supports a wide range of storage systems, network interfaces, and specialized mainframe hardware through its modular kernel design.  Linux handles the hardware compatibility at the OS level.
These technical advantages make Linux an ideal bridge between legacy application requirements and modern mainframe hardware capabilities, providing the stability and compatibility that mission-critical legacy systems demand.
(Q4) Explain the structure of the Linux File System with proper diagram. 
           
             The Linux file system is organized as an inverted tree structure that starts from a single root directory (/) and branches out into subdirectories ,unlike Windows (which has C:, D: drives).
           
           System Boot & Core (/boot, /bin, /sbin, /lib)

    • Purpose: Files needed to start and run the system 
    • Access: Root required for modifications 
    • Example: When you turn on your computer, files from /boot load the kernel
      Configuration (/etc)
    • Purpose: System-wide settings and configuration files 
    • Format: Usually plain text files 
    • Example: /etc/passwd contains user account information 
      User Space (/home, /root)
    • Purpose: Personal directories for users 
    • Permissions: Each user owns their home directory 
    • Example: /home/john/Documents is John's document folder
      Hardware Interface (/dev, /sys, /proc)
    • Purpose: Interface with hardware and kernel 
    • Special: These are virtual files (not stored on disk) 
    • Example: /dev/sda1 represents your first hard drive partition
      Programs & Libraries (/usr, /opt)
    • Purpose: User applications and supporting libraries 
    • Structure: /usr mirrors root structure (/usr/bin, /usr/lib, etc.) 
    • Example: Your text editor is likely in /usr/bin/ 
      Variable Data (/var, /tmp)
    • Purpose: Files that change frequently 
    • Examples: Log files, temporary files, mail storage 
    • Cleanup: /tmp is often cleared on reboot
       DIAGRAM:
                                 ROOT DIRECTORY (Starting point)
├── bin/                   Essential user commands (ls, cat, cp)
├── boot/                  Boot files (kernel, bootloader)
├── dev/                   Device files (sda1, tty, null)
├── etc/                   System configuration files
├── home/                  User home directories
│   ├── alice/
│   └── bob/
├── lib/                    Essential system libraries
├── media/                 Removable media mount points
├── opt/                   Optional third-party software
├── proc/                  Process & kernel info (virtual)
├── root/                  Root user's home directory
├── sbin/                  System administration commands
├── tmp/                   Temporary files (cleared on reboot)
├── usr/                   User programs and data
│   ├── bin/              User commands (firefox, vim)
│   ├── lib/              Program libraries
│   └── local/            Locally installed software
└── var/                   Variable data (logs, cache, mail)
    ├── log/              System logs
    └── www/              Web server file


