(Q10)  A system administrator accidentally powers off a Server machine without shutting it down properly. What problems can occur to the said Server?
    
  File System Corruption
    • Incomplete file writes - Files being saved become corrupted or lost 
    • File system inconsistencies - File allocation tables and metadata become damaged 
    • Lost data - Recent file changes stored in memory buffers are permanently lost 
Database Corruption
    • Incomplete transactions - Database transactions in progress left in inconsistent state 
    • Corrupted indexes - Database indexes become damaged, causing query failures 
    • Data inconsistency - Related data across tables becomes mismatched 
Hardware Damage
    • Hard drive damage - Read/write heads may not park properly, causing disk damage 
    • Power supply stress - Sudden shutdown can damage power components 
System Boot Problems
    • Boot failures - System may fail to start due to corrupted system files 
    • Long boot times - Forced file system check (fsck) can take hours 
    • Service startup failures - Critical services may fail to start properly 
Data Loss
    • Recent modifications lost - Any unsaved changes in memory are permanently lost 
    • Log file corruption - System and application logs become incomplete 
    • User session data lost - Active user work and sessions are terminated abruptly 
Recovery Impact
    • Extended downtime - File system repair and service recovery takes significant time 
    • Potential data restoration - May require restoring from backups 
    • Manual reconfiguration - Services may need manual setup to work properly 


